sequenceDiagram
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task, R1-R4, single-swamp-node (@swamp/ssh from 209), DECISIONS 1 (token minted at admission, vault.get), 2 (DNS held), Schedules native (Nick 2026-11-02). docs/reference/instance-removal.md.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:destroy
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>serve on Node 209
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: instance:destroy 42<br/>Instance id · --yes --force
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:destroy 42 --yes`
  %%> - syntax: `<instance> [--yes] [--force] [--idempotency-key=KEY] [--json]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Instance's id, as `instance:list` shows it
  %%> Confirmation:
  %%> - without `--yes`, the CLI asks first; the default is No
  %%> - without a terminal, or with `--json`, `--yes` is required
  %%> With `--force`:
  %%> - also deletes dirty or unpublished source, and a checkout with its linked worktrees
  %%> - it never waives the source identity checks, and it is not consent
  Note over GW: Validate, capture the set<br/>Instance 42 · task #5132
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - your Node has an access grant to Node 12, which runs Instance 42
  %%> - Instance 42 is a development Instance; production removal is not charted here
  %%> - no other lifecycle change and no unfinished transfer holds Instance 42
  %%> Captures:
  %%> - the source: path, owner, origin, branch, linked worktrees
  %%> - what Instance 42 owns: Processes, Schedules, Route, SQLite files
  %%> - the Project teardown steps
  %%> Result:
  %%> - Instance 42 is marked removing, with the captured set
  %%> - task #5132 recorded with the full intent
  GW->>SW: Remove Schedule triggers<br/>workflow trigger removal
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes the native Swamp schedule trigger of each Schedule of Instance 42
  %%> - there is no Node work here; Schedules install nothing on Nodes
  %%> Why first:
  %%> - no new fire starts against a checkout that is being removed
  %%> Running fire:
  %%> - a fire that already started runs to its end, within its own timeout, and is still recorded
  opt not forced, private repo
    GW->>SW: Push the token<br/>vault: github-token
    %%> Why:
    %%> - a normal removal checks that the work is pushed, which needs a fetch from origin
    %%> - only the Gateway holds the GitHub App key
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - mints a read token for this one repository, valid 1 hour, and pushes it into Swamp's vault
    %%> Not needed:
    %%> - with `--force`, or for a public repository
  end
  GW->>SW: Start run, full intent<br/>operation-instance-destroy
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - starts one run of `operation-instance-destroy` on Swamp serve (Node 209)
  %%> Inputs:
  %%> - Node 12's address and SSH host keys; the SSH identity comes from the `swamp` role
  %%> - the captured set, the teardown steps, the Node's managed user
  %%> - secrets only as vault key names
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task #5132
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task number, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>N: Verify the source<br/>@swamp/ssh · owner, origin, inode
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks that the checkout and its linked worktrees are the ones captured at admission
  %%> - same path, owner, origin and filesystem identity
  %%> Refuses:
  %%> - another checkout, owner or Node, also with `--force`
  %%> - a symlink in place of the checkout
  opt not forced
    SW->>N: Check work is pushed<br/>vault.get token · fetch, compare
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - fetches origin with the vault token and compares
    %%> Refuses:
    %%> - dirty source
    %%> - a `HEAD` that no origin branch or tag contains
    %%> - linked worktrees on the checkout
    %%> Result of a refusal:
    %%> - the run stops here and nothing changes
  end
  SW->>N: Stop, remove Processes<br/>systemd units · containers
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - stops each Process of Instance 42 and removes its unit or container
  %%> Why before teardown:
  %%> - nothing still writes to the application while teardown runs
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - Processes of other Instances on Node 12
  SW->>N: Run teardown steps<br/>@swamp/ssh · own timeout each
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - runs the Project's teardown steps in order, as the Node's managed user, from the repository root
  %%> Limits:
  %%> - each step has its own timeout
  %%> Failure:
  %%> - a failed step fails the run; the next `instance:destroy` runs the whole list again
  SW->>N: Remove SQLite files<br/>owned files only
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - deletes the SQLite files that Instance 42 owns
  %%> Not included:
  %%> - other databases; their records go with the Instance, nothing on the Database server changes
  SW->>N: Withdraw the Route<br/>Caddy site, PHP pool, cert
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes the Caddy site of Instance 42 and reloads Caddy; no restart
  %%> - removes its PHP pool and its certificate
  %%> Keeps:
  %%> - every other site, pool and certificate on Node 12
  Note over SW: DNS withdrawal held<br/>open: Nick decides
  %%> Open:
  %%> - DNS publication for Routes is on hold (`native-dns-takeover-held`)
  %%> - until Nick decides, the run removes no DNS record
  SW->>N: Remove the source<br/>worktrees first, checkout last
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - removes linked worktrees, then the checkout itself
  %%> - deletes only the paths whose identity it verified
  %%> Runs as:
  %%> - the Node's own user, not root
  SW->>N: Check it is gone<br/>path, units, Caddy site absent
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - checks that the checkout, the Process units and the Caddy site are absent
  %%> Why:
  %%> - Instance 42 only counts as removed when the Node shows it
  alt all steps pass
    N-->>SW: Instance 42 absent
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the check passed
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Delete Instance, records<br/>task succeeded
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Instance 42 is deleted, with its Processes, Schedules and Route records
    %%> - owned database records are deleted
    %%> - closed transfer history stays, without its Instance link
    %%> - task #5132 succeeds
  else a step fails
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or refusal
    %%> When:
    %%> - a source check refuses, a teardown step fails, or something is still there
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>error_code, failed step
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code
    Note over GW: Keep Instance, mark failed<br/>rerun the same destroy
    %%> Result:
    %%> - Instance 42 and its records stay, marked removal failed
    %%> - task #5132 fails
    %%> On the Node:
    %%> - what was already removed stays removed; no rollback
    %%> - the Schedule triggers stay removed
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run `instance:destroy 42` again; it is a new task and converges the same captured set
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show #5132
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
